          "n43001001017"
        ],
        "text": "Ἐν ἀρχῇ ἦν ὁ λόγος, καὶ ὁ λόγος ἦν πρὸς τὸν θεόν, καὶ θεὸς ἦν ὁ λόγος."
      },
      "literal_gloss": "In beginning was the word, and the word was toward the God, and God was the word.",
      "renderings": {
        "en": {
          "L": "In [the] beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God.",
          "B": "In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God.",
          "R": "In the beginning the Word was already there. The Word was with God, and the Word was God."
        }
      },
      "decisions": [
        {
          "id": "m1",
          "layer": "meaning",
          "language": null,
          "category": "allusion",
          "tokens": [
            "n43001001001",
            "n43001001002"
          ],
          "choice": "In the beginning",
          "alternatives": [
            "At the start",
            "Before time"
          ],
          "reason": "The same two words open Genesis 1:1 in the Greek Old Testament. John is retelling creation, so every level keeps the echo.",
          "uncertain": false,
          "footnote": true
        },
        {
          "id": "m2",
          "layer": "meaning",
          "language": null,
          "category": "word_sense",
          "tokens": [
            "n43001001004",
            "n43001001005",
            "n43001001007",
            "n43001001008",
            "n43001001016",
            "n43001001017"
          ],
          "choice": "the Word",
          "alternatives": [
            "the Message",
            "Reason",
            "the Logos"
          ],
          "reason": "Key term. In Genesis 1 God creates by speaking; \"Word\" keeps that link. \"Reason\" imports Greek philosophy John does not develop.",
          "uncertain": false,
          "footnote": true
        },
        {
          "id": "m3",
          "layer": "meaning",
          "language": null,
          "category": "grammar",
          "tokens": [
            "n43001001010",
            "n43001001011",
            "n43001001012"
          ],
          "choice": "with God",
          "alternatives": [
            "toward God",
            "face to face with God"
          ],
          "reason": "πρός with the accusative normally means \"toward\"; here it describes presence and relationship. \"With\" is natural English, and the note carries the fuller sense.",
          "uncertain": false,
          "footnote": true
        },
        {
          "id": "m4",
          "layer": "meaning",
          "language": null,
          "category": "theology",
          "tokens": [
            "n43001001014",
            "n43001001015",
            "n43001001016",
            "n43001001017"
          ],
          "choice": "the Word was God",
          "alternatives": [
            "the Word was a god",
            "the Word was divine"
          ],
          "reason": "ὁ λόγος has the article, so it is the subject. θεός comes first without the article and says what the Word is, without making the Word the same person as \"the God\" just mentioned. \"A god\" would contradict John's insistence on one God; \"divine\" is weaker than the noun.",
          "uncertain": false,
          "footnote": true
        },
        {
          "id": "r-en-1",
          "layer": "rendering",
          "language": "en",
          "levels": [
            "R"
          ],
          "category": "word_choice",
          "tokens": [
            "n43001001003"
          ],
          "choice": "was already there",
          "alternatives": [
            "was"
          ],
          "reason": "The imperfect ἦν (was, continuing) contrasts with ἐγένετο (came into being) in verses 3, 6 and 14. Readable makes the contrast explicit.",
          "uncertain": false,
          "footnote": false
        }
      ],
